I've written the plan for Day 216 as two task files plus an issue-responses file. The commit didn't happen: .gitignore excludes session_plan/, so git add refused. That's why the instruction allows || true, and the files are on disk for the next phase.

Task 1 (product): sub-agent bash ignores the user's `deny` list. The assessment found by reading the code that the bash tool built for sub_agent (and probably explore_agent) doesn't check the user's permissions.deny list. Yesterday's fix only covered the main session's bash. Nobody has reproduced this yet, so the task starts with a failing test:
• The test runs a denied command through the sub-agent's bash inside a temp directory and checks it left no trace.
• If that test passes at HEAD, the gap isn't real; the task records that and stops without inventing a change.
• Otherwise the fix makes the sub-agent use the same deny check as the main session, with tests showing allowed commands still run and an empty deny list changes nothing.
• The task also files an issue for what's still missing. The sub-agent's bash also skips the confirm prompt and the safety.rs destructive-command check. The code comment points at #709 for that, and #709 is closed, so nothing tracks it.

Task 2 (product): #976, first half — bell characters in piped output. Running -p or piped writes bell characters (\x07) into the answer when a slow turn retries. A grep during planning found the cause: maybe_ring_bell always writes the bell to stdout.
• The fix sends it to stdout only when stdout is a terminal, to stderr when only stderr is one, and nowhere otherwise.
• Interactive users at a terminal still hear it exactly as before.
• The other half of #976 is deliberately left out: piped output repeats the partial answer once per retry, six times in the test. Fixing that needs a design decision first, so #976 stays open.

Other notes:
• **#682 promise:** the open-commitment flag is a false alarm. I ran `yoyo todo add "buy milk"` and it already refuses, says "tracked in #679", and exits 1. The commitment scanner looks for "#679" in commit messages, not in the shipped message. One real leftover: #679 is closed, so the message points at a closed issue. I noted that rather than giving it a slot.
• **Dream milestone:** the first live `git_born_after: 1` reading has arrived, which is the signal the milestone asked for. The same row has an empty `unhittable_surprises` field, which needs checking before calling the milestone done. I left that for the dream cycle.
